<p>Starting to write can feel strangely difficult because the idea arrives at full size. You imagine the finished essay, novel, memoir, article, or book. Then you look at the empty page and ask the first paragraph to prove that you are capable of all of it. That is a heavy job for one sentence.</p>
<p>A better beginning is smaller and more practical. You do not need a perfect idea, a special room, or an hour that feels entirely free. You need a subject that gives you a little energy, a modest container for your attention, and a way to leave the page with a next step waiting for you.</p>
<p>This guide is for people who want to begin writing but are tired of treating every session as a test. The six steps below will help you find material, make a rough start, and build enough continuity for the work to become easier to return to.</p>
<h2>1. Choose a subject that already has some weight</h2>
<p>Do not begin by asking what topic would impress someone. Begin with what has been asking for your attention. It might be a family story, a book you disagree with, a neighborhood you remember, a change at work, a tool that changed how you think, or a question you cannot settle. The material does not need to be dramatic. It needs to give you something real to observe.</p>
<p>Try making a list of ten possible subjects without judging them. Next to each one, write the smallest detail you can recall: the smell of a room, a sentence someone said, the color of an old coat, the moment an argument turned, the object left behind after a visit. Details make the page less abstract. They also help you see which subject has enough life to carry you past the first few lines.</p>
<p>Patrick&#x27;s <a href="/books/blurred-lines">Blurred Lines</a> offers one useful model for this kind of attention. It stays with questions of culture and identity that become clearer when they are considered through lived detail instead of easy labels. Your subject does not have to be your life story. It simply needs to be close enough for you to have a point of view.</p>
<picture><source srcset="/images/blog/start-writing-subject-18a0bad834575f57.webp" type="image/webp"/><img src="/images/blog/start-writing-subject-5514967c55ca2335.jpg" alt="Blank notebook, pencil, paper, ticket, and leaf arranged on a wood desk" loading="lazy" decoding="async"/></picture>
<h2>2. Give yourself a small and definite writing session</h2>
<p>“I should write more” is a direction, not a plan. Make the first session specific: twenty minutes after breakfast, one page before bed, three paragraphs at the library, or a short notebook entry while the coffee is brewing. A clear time and place reduce the negotiation that happens when you are already tired.</p>
<p>There is no universal number of days that turns writing into an automatic habit. The often-cited habit research by <a href="https://doi.org/10.1002/ejsp.674" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Phillippa Lally and colleagues</a> found that people build routines at very different speeds. The useful lesson is not to force a streak. It is to make the return easy enough that you can repeat it in the real conditions of your life.</p>
<p>Keep the promise modest at first. Fifteen focused minutes is enough to make a paragraph, sketch an opening, or list the questions a piece needs to answer. When you finish, stop with a little left in the tank. Leaving before you are completely exhausted can make tomorrow&#x27;s return feel possible.</p>
<p>For a deeper approach to shaping this rhythm, read <a href="/blog/creator-practice/build-creative-practice">How to Build a Creative Practice That Lasts</a>. It helps turn a vague desire to make things into a practice that can survive a busy week.</p>
<h2>3. Start with a question, not a grand conclusion</h2>
<p>A blank page gets intimidating when you think you must know the final answer before you begin. Instead, write a question at the top. What do I actually remember about this moment? Why did that conversation stay with me? What changed after I learned this? What am I not seeing clearly yet? A good question gives the draft a direction without making it pretend to be finished.</p>
<p>Then write three plain answers or observations underneath it. Do not worry about transitions. Do not open with a sweeping statement about the world. Give yourself material first. You can connect and refine it later, once you have something concrete to work with.</p>
<p>Planning can help, especially for a longer piece, but it should remain light. An outline with a question, three points, and one example is enough to begin. Research on <a href="https://doi.org/10.1037/0003-066X.54.7.493" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">implementation intentions</a> makes the same practical point: deciding in advance what you will do, and when, gives a good intention a better chance of becoming action.</p>
<h2>4. Make the first draft private, rough, and useful</h2>
<p>The first draft is allowed to be awkward. In fact, it is supposed to be incomplete. Its job is not to demonstrate your talent. Its job is to give you something you can examine. If you try to make every sentence final while you are still discovering the subject, you will spend most of the session standing at the door.</p>
<p>Set a timer and write without correcting every line. When you do not know what comes next, describe the last thing you can see or ask another question in the draft. You can write, “What I mean is...” or “The part I keep avoiding is...” and continue from there. These are not sentences you need to keep. They are handles that let you move the work forward.</p>
<picture><source srcset="/images/blog/start-writing-first-draft-30556ab0b16bedc0.webp" type="image/webp"/><img src="/images/blog/start-writing-first-draft-48afa347eb3d4179.jpg" alt="Rough handwritten draft, pencil, timer, and folded paper on a desk" loading="lazy" decoding="async"/></picture>
<p>Separate making from judging. During the first pass, collect scenes, facts, phrases, and questions. During the second, decide what belongs. That separation is one of the central lessons in <a href="/books/iq-an-overrated-quotient">IQ: An Overrated Quotient</a>, where creative instinct becomes more useful when it meets patient structure.</p>
<p>Try a simple rule: do not revise until you have filled one page or spent the full twenty minutes. Your first version may be too long, too cautious, too vague, or headed in the wrong direction. Good. Now you have a real problem to solve, which is much better than an imaginary one.</p>
<h2>5. Read back for the part that feels alive</h2>
<p>After a short break, read your draft once without changing anything. Underline the line that surprises you, the detail that feels most specific, and the sentence that makes you curious to continue. Those marks show you where the piece has energy. They are often a better guide than the first idea you had about what the writing should be.</p>
<p>On the next pass, look for three things. First, where have you said the same point twice? Cut one version. Second, where is the language too general? Replace one broad statement with a scene, example, or image. Third, where does the reader need a small bridge between ideas? Add only enough explanation to make the movement clear.</p>
<p>Reading aloud helps because your ear notices what your eye has become used to. If a sentence makes you lose your place or sound unlike yourself, mark it and move on. Revision is less punishing when it is treated as listening. The goal is not to make every paragraph grand. It is to make the reader able to follow the thought.</p>
<p>Writers who are also trying to develop a more recognizable style can continue with <a href="/blog/creator-practice/find-your-writing-voice">How to Find Your Writing Voice: 7 Exercises</a>. Voice is not a costume you add to a clean draft. It emerges when you make repeated, attentive choices about what to say and how to say it.</p>
<h2>Choose a first project you can finish</h2>
<p>Beginning does not require a book-length idea. A short personal essay, a review of a book that stayed with you, a letter you may never send, or a scene from a day you remember clearly can teach you more about writing than a giant project that remains too large to touch. Finishing a small piece gives you the full experience: choosing, drafting, cutting, and deciding when the work has said enough.</p>
<p>Keep the project narrow enough to hold in your mind. If you are writing about a move, write about the first night in the new room. If you are writing about a person, choose one conversation. If you are writing about a belief, begin with the moment it became difficult. You can always discover a larger piece inside the smaller one. It is much harder to find the smaller piece after you have made the task too broad to begin.</p>
<h2>6. Leave a clear path back to the work</h2>
<p>The end of a writing session matters as much as the beginning. Before you close the notebook or document, write one sentence that tells tomorrow&#x27;s self what to do next. It could be “Describe the kitchen before the argument,” “Find the example that proves this,” “Move the second paragraph to the opening,” or “Write the part I am avoiding.”</p>
<p>This tiny note prevents the work from becoming a fog overnight. Instead of returning to a blank page, you return to a visible door. It also gives you permission to stop. You do not have to keep writing until you are empty. You only need to preserve the thread.</p>
<picture><source srcset="/images/blog/start-writing-revision-1c45b3b38a47c6d9.webp" type="image/webp"/><img src="/images/blog/start-writing-revision-313302ae882edb3e.jpg" alt="Manuscript pages, pencil, open book, and desk lamp on a wooden desk at dusk" loading="lazy" decoding="async"/></picture>
<p>As the work grows, keep a short record of what you learned. Note what helped you begin, what distracted you, and what question became more interesting. This turns each session into material for the next one. It is also a humane alternative to measuring yourself by word count alone.</p>
<p>For readers drawn to the discipline of continuing through difficulty, <a href="/books/the-dogged-spirit-of-success">The Dogged Spirit of Success</a> explores persistence as a way of protecting meaningful work, not as a reason to punish yourself. Starting is important. Returning is what lets the work take shape.</p>
<h2>Let the work begin before you feel ready</h2>
<p>Writing becomes less mysterious when you stop demanding that the beginning carry the entire project. Choose a subject with some weight. Make a small appointment with it. Start from a question. Draft without trying to impress anyone. Read for the sentence that feels alive. Leave a note that makes the next return easier.</p>
<p>The six steps are simple because beginning needs fewer obstacles, not more. A body of work is built from ordinary sessions that accumulate: a page marked up, a scene remembered, a question sharpened, a draft made clearer than it was yesterday.</p>
<p>Patrick Okeke&#x27;s <a href="/books">books</a> are for readers who want to stay with those questions, from identity and culture to influence, technology, and the craft of making useful things. You can also <a href="/contact">write to Patrick</a> about the work you are beginning.</p>
<section class="article-faq"><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<div><h3>What is the easiest way to start writing?</h3>
<p>Choose one small subject you genuinely care about, set a short timer, and write a rough first version without trying to make it complete. A scene, memory, question, object, or disagreement is enough. The goal of the first session is to leave a trace you can return to, not to produce a finished piece.</p>
</div>
<div><h3>How long should a beginner write each day?</h3>
<p>Start with an amount that fits your life and is easy to repeat. Fifteen or twenty focused minutes is enough for a useful session. A modest habit that survives ordinary weeks will teach you more than an ambitious schedule that becomes a source of guilt.</p>
</div>
<div><h3>What should I write about when I have no ideas?</h3>
<p>Begin with material already close to you. Write about a recent conversation, an object on your desk, a book that unsettled you, a place you know well, or a question that keeps returning. Specific material gives you something to notice before you need to invent anything.</p>
</div>
<div><h3>Should I plan before I start writing?</h3>
<p>A small plan helps, but it should not delay the draft. Write down the question you are trying to answer, three things you want to include, and the next scene or example. That is usually enough structure to begin while leaving room for the work to show you what it needs.</p>
</div>
